  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1252 PM CDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\nTemperatures remain warm and Summer-like this afternoon with \ncontinued southerly flow over much of the Great Plains. Thick high \ncirrus associated with a passing disturbance will limit the amount \nof mixing that can be achieved through this afternoon. This keeps \nwind speeds and temperatures lower than they might have otherwise \nbeen. That said, well above normal temperatures in the upper 80s and \nlow 90s with peak gusts of 25-30 MPH can be expected.\n\nAs part of the remnants of Rachel eject northeast across the Rocky \nMountains tonight, an area of low pressure begins to develop over \nWyoming and northeastern Colorado. This feature helps a cold front \nsink south into the area. Unfortunately, this front stalls not too \nfar into northeast Nebraska. South of the front, continued warm \nadvection and moisture return keeps overnight temperatures in the \nupper 60s to low 70s. Slightly cooler temperatures can be expected \nbehind the front over northeast Nebraska. \n\nThe cold front remains nearly stationary on Monday. Daytime heating \nmay even lift the front back north slightly. As warm, moist \nsoutherly to southwesterly flow continues, expected one last day of \nwidespread 80s and even low 90s. An upper-level low pressure moves \ninto the Great Basin Monday night. This redevelops low pressure over \neastern Colorado. The cold front finally sags south through most of \nthe region in response. The cold front will become nearly stationary \nonce again over southeast Nebraska and southwest Iowa though. Warm \nadvection and weak ascent ahead of the approaching system may help \nto initiate a few showers near the front during this time. That \nsaid, significant dry air in the lowest levels of the atmosphere \nkeeps precipitation light. \n\nBy Tuesday, the previously mentioned upper-level low slowly shifts \neast towards the Four Corners region. At the same time, remnants of \nSimon are forecast to eject northeast towards the area. These \nfeatures are expected to result in shower and thunderstorm \ndevelopment across much of the central Plains Tuesday afternoon and \nevening. With the front mostly through the area, initial rain \nchances may be confined to southeast Nebraska and southwest Iowa. \nThere remains some potential for more widespread showers and \nthunderstorms Tuesday evening with a few models lifting the front \nback north towards the Interstate 80 corridor. In this event, \ngreater rain chances and even some severe weather may be possible. \nIn either case, as the upper-low continues its slow trek east, \nexpect increasing rain chances across the entire region heading into \nWednesday and Thursday. Temperatures cool significantly during this \ntime due to a combination of cooler temperatures behind the front, \nrain, and increased cloud cover. Temperatures in the 70s to low 80s \non Tuesday fall into the mid 60s by Thursday.\n\nFall-like weather is set to return by week's end into next weekend. \nA strong area of high pressure sinks south from Canada into the \nGreat Plains. This brings much cooler air along with it. There \nremains uncertainty on just how cold temperatures may get, but we \ncan anticipate 50s and 60s for afternoon highs and 40s to even 30s \nfor overnight lows. If trends become even colder, there may be the \npotential for a light frost or freeze this weekend.",
